tContentLines` then walks the slice again to split newlines (`viewport.go:241-253`). Bursty end-of-run event floods will rebuild the whole buffer on each event (O(n) per event, O(n^2) per burst). Most agents buffer their output so this rarely fires, but if a burst lands while the user is in detail mode the redraw will visibly stutter. Consider building incrementally (append new lines to existing slice) or batching multiple `agentEventMsg`s before refreshing.\n\n5. **`wrapDisplayWidth(\"\", width)` returns `nil`** — `tui_text.go:67-83`. Combined with `eventLines` for any unknown event type returning `wrapDisplayWidth(raw, maxWidth)` where `raw` is \"[unknown event]\" (non-empty), this is fine. But if a future event variant produced an empty `raw` string by oversight, that event would contribute zero lines to the viewport with no indication. Add a comment in `eventLines` documenting that every branch must produce non-empty `raw` (the current `default` does).\n\n6. **Bubble Tea program error swallowed** — `tui_sink.go:83-89`. The comment justifies swallowing as \"non-actionable in background goroutine,\" but for diagnostic value it should log to the `.entire/logs/` debug log via `logging.Debug(ctx, \"review tui program exited with error\", \"err\", err)`. Six months from now, a terminal-resize race or transport error here is invisible.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- `cancelOnce` correctness verified — test `SecondCtrlCForceQuits` (`tui_model_test.go:518-542`) pins exactly-once invocation across two Ctrl+Cs.\n- MouseMode reset on exit from detail mode is implicit but correct — `tui_model.go:427-432` only sets `v.MouseMode` when `m.detailMode`, so the dashboard frame returns `MouseModeNone` and the cursed_renderer (vendored at `cursed_renderer.go:350-353`) actively disables mouse capture on the transition. Test at `tui_model_test.go:216-218` pins it. Issue 6 is handled.\n- No `signal.Notify` goroutine reintroduced (`grep` confirms only a comment at `tui_sink.go:30`). The CLAUDE.m